  :backend-parity
  {:disposition :implemented-partial
   :current "The recorded bounded language slices now run on compiler and primary Wasm/CLJS; representation and performance contracts remain deliberately narrower than Clojure."
   ;; What the cited evidence is, measured 2026-08-12 by reading it.
   ;;
   ;; `:primary-test` is not a parity test. Its suite deftest is named
   ;; `authority-positive-cases-run-on-primary-wasm`: it compiles each case,
   ;; runs it through `wasm-exec/run-main` on ONE backend, and compares the
   ;; result to the `(:kotoba expect)` literal recorded in the manifest. It
   ;; never runs a case on a second backend, so it cannot witness parity
   ;; between two.
   ;;
   ;; Corrected 2026-08-12, same day: the first version of this audit also said
   ;; a case that fails to compile asserts nothing, reading the
   ;; `(when (:kotoba.wasm/ok? wasm) ...)` guard in `run-case` as the whole
   ;; story. It is not — `compile-source` and `compile-run-case` each carry
   ;; their own `(is (:kotoba.wasm/ok? wasm) ...)`, so a compile failure does
   ;; fail the suite. The guard only suppresses a second, cascading failure on
   ;; the result comparison.
   ;;
   ;; What IS uncovered is narrower and countable. Measured against the
   ;; authority manifest: 37 cases, 34 of kind :run/:compile-expr, 32 carrying
   ;; a `:kotoba` expect, and 31 reaching a result assertion.
   ;;
   ;; Corrected a second time, 2026-08-12. The first list of uncovered cases
   ;; named `:entry-extension-clj` and `:entry-extension-cljs` as "missing a
   ;; :kotoba expect", which read as a defect in the cases. It is not. Those
   ;; two declare `:required-backends #{:host-clj}` and `#{:host-cljs}` and
   ;; carry `{:clj 25}` / `{:cljs 45}` — the whole point of the
   ;; `entry_extensions` family is that the extension selects the HOST, and a
   ;; `.clj` entry is not supposed to have a Kotoba result. Adding a `:kotoba`
   ;; expect there would assert something the case was written to deny.
   ;;
   ;; The gap is a missing runner, not a missing expect. `:host-clj` and
   ;; `:host-cljs` are declared backends in the manifest
   ;; (`:status :host-compat`), 7 of the 37 cases require one of them, and no
   ;; runner in kotoba, kotoba-lang or the compiler executes either — searched
   ;; src and test in all three, zero hits. So 13 recorded `:clj`/`:cljs`
   ;; expect values are never compared against anything, and the two cases that
   ;; require ONLY a host backend are executed by nothing at all.
   ;;
   ;; This is the mechanism behind `:cljs-parity-coverage :none` below: the
   ;; manifest declares a CLJS host, cases carry `:cljs` expects, and nothing
   ;; runs them. Building those two runners is what would make `:kotoba-cljs`
   ;; entailed by a case rather than asserted by hand.
   ;;
   ;; Real dual-backend execution does exist, in the compiler repo rather than
   ;; here: `resources/kotoba/lang-conformance/pilot-manifest.edn`, 60 cases,
   ;; every one `:required-backends #{:kir :wasm32-kotoba-v1}`, run by
   ;; `kotoba.compiler.lang-conformance` and asserted equal across both
   ;; backends per case. Only 2 of the 16 `:shared-cases` below also appear
   ;; there. Nothing covers `:kotoba-cljs` against `:kotoba-wasm`, and the two
   ;; vocabularies do not intersect — see `:implementation-vocabulary`.
   ;;
   ;; This entry states a three-backend claim and has no `:implementation` and
   ;; no `:conformance` key of its own, so it is not subject to the portable
   ;; claim gate it purports to underwrite.
   :evidence-audit {:as-of "2026-08-12"
                    :primary-test-is-single-backend true
                    :compile-failure-fails-the-suite true
                    :authority-manifest-cases 37
                    :cases-reaching-a-result-assertion 31
                    :host-runners {:host-clj "kotoba.lang.host-clj-conformance-test, 2026-08-12; all 6 cases requiring :host-clj"
                                   :host-cljs "kotoba.lang.host-cljs-conformance-test + scripts/host_cljs_conformance.cljs, 2026-08-12; all 6 cases requiring :host-cljs, on nbb"}
                    :cases-no-runner-executes []
                    :clj-expect-values {:total 7 :compared 6
                                        :uncompared [:inline-expression-compile]}
                    :cljs-expect-values {:total 7 :compared 6
                                         :uncompared [:inline-expression-compile]}
                    :backends-not-examined-here [:compiler-admit :native-x86_64 :native-aarch64]
                    :count-correction "An earlier revision of this audit said 13 host expect values. Counted: 7 :clj and 7 :cljs. It also implied every case carrying a host expect requires a host backend; :inline-expression-compile carries :clj and :cljs expects and requires neither."
                    :found-by-running-them [":namespace-extension-priority :function was \"main\" where the entry defines run"
                                            ":namespace-extension-priority :clj was 11 where the Clojure loader gives 2"]
                    :real-dual-backend "compiler resources/kotoba/lang-conformance/pilot-manifest.edn (60 cases, :kir + :wasm32-kotoba-v1)"
                    :shared-cases-also-in-that-manifest 2
                    :cljs-parity-coverage
                    {:before :none
                     :now "6 of the 7 recorded :cljs expects are compared on a real ClojureScript host. This is per-case execution, not cross-backend comparison: no harness yet runs one program on :kotoba-wasm and :kotoba-cljs and compares the two results to each other."}}
   :evidence {:primary-test kotoba.language-conformance-test
              :shared-cases [:reader-target-branching :reader-target-default-fallback
                             :reader-conditional-splicing :entry-extension-kotoba
                             :entry-extension-cljk :entry-extension-cljc
                             :inline-expression-compile
                             :bounded-set-literal-and-operations
                             :bounded-vector-literal-and-operations
                             :bounded-named-higher-order-collections
                             :nested-let-destructuring
                             :record-protocol-static-dispatch
                             :named-multi-arity-and-variadic-functions
                             :first-class-closures-invoke-and-apply
                             :canonical-bytes-closure-result
                             :pure-call-by-name-lazy-sequences]}}}

 :classification-rule
 {:default-for-missing :not-yet-implemented
  :security-constraint-requires [:named-invariant :fail-closed-enforcement :adr]
  ;; How each of those three is checked, so the requirement is not a claim the
  ;; file makes about itself. All three run in `grammar-authority/validate` and
  ;; are pinned by `test/kotoba/lang/grammar_authority_test.clj`.
  :security-constraint-checks
  {:named-invariant "the entry key, plus :forbidden/unclassified-by-surface — every forbidden head must be classified by some invariant"
   :fail-closed-enforcement ":forbidden/security-surface-not-forbidden — every form a security constraint names must be in guest-grammar :forbidden-heads, which the compiler unions into its own set via the vendored catalog"
   :adr ":security/constraint-missing-adr and :security/adr-path-unresolved"}
  ;; Portable claims that name no conformance case at all. Such an entry is
  ;; checked by nothing: `portable-without-evidence` requires a `:conformance`
  ;; before it looks at anything, and `conformance-matrix/validate-claims` only
  ;; validates links that exist — so claiming all three backends and naming no
  ;; case is strictly less checked than naming one badly.
  ;;
  ;; This register makes the debt a ratchet rather than a wall. A claim listed
  ;; here is grandfathered; one that is not fails
  ;; `:portable/claim-without-conformance`. An entry that gains a link must be
  ;; removed, or `:portable/conformance-debt-stale` fails — so the list shrinks
  ;; as work lands and cannot quietly grow.
  ;;
  ;; Measured 2026-08-12: 18 entries qualify as portable claims
  ;; (`portable-backends ⊆ :implementation`), 8 carry a link, these 10 do not.
  ;; Nothing here is a statement that the feature is unsupported — only that no
  ;; conformance case is named for it.
  ;; The tokens `:implementation` sets are allowed to contain. This freezes the
  ;; current ten so a new one cannot appear silently; it does NOT bless them.
  ;;
  ;; Measured 2026-08-12: this is a third, informal backend namespace. Two real
  ;; closed vocabularies exist and neither shares a single token with it —
  ;; `kotoba-kir/src/kotoba/kir/target.cljc` `compatibility-targets`
  ;; (:wasm32-kotoba-v1 :x86_64-kotoba-v1 :aarch64-kotoba-v1 :cljs-kotoba-v1
  ;; :js-kotoba-v1) and `lang/conformance/manifest.edn` `:backends`
  ;; (:kir :wasm32-kotoba-v1 :js-kotoba-v1 :native-x86_64 :native-aarch64
  ;; :host-clj :host-cljs :compiler-admit). Before this entry nothing defined,
  ;; enumerated or constrained these tokens: `surface-matrix/backends-of` joins
  ;; whatever set it finds into a markdown column, so any keyword rendered.
  ;;
  ;; Consequence, and the reason `:backend-parity-evidence` is still unchecked:
  ;; a linked conformance case declares `:required-backends #{:kir
  ;; :wasm32-kotoba-v1}`, and there is no expressible comparison between that
  ;; and an `:implementation` of #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs}. So
  ;; `:kotoba-cljs`, asserted on 19 entries, is entailed by no case anywhere.
  ;; Reconciling the three namespaces is a decision about the authority, not
  ;; something a check can take — it is recorded here rather than guessed at.
  :implementation-vocabulary
  {:as-of "2026-08-12"
   :status :unverified-assertion
   :tokens #{:compiler :kotoba-wasm :kotoba-cljs :kir :wasm32 :kotoba-kir
             :restricted-esm :compiler-host-jvm :compiler-host-cljs
             :compiler-native-host}
   :counts {:compiler 20 :kotoba-wasm 20 :kotoba-cljs 19 :kir 2 :wasm32 2
            :kotoba-kir 1 :restricted-esm 1 :compiler-host-jvm 1
            :compiler-host-cljs 1 :compiler-native-host 1}
   :granularity-note "Mixed. :kotoba-wasm and :wasm32 co-occur on two entries; :kir and :kotoba-kir are two spellings; :compiler-native-host was coined for one entry (:native-homogeneous-vectors) and is defined nowhere."
   :rule "A token outside this set fails :portable/unknown-implementation-token. Adding one means deciding what it means and where its evidence lives."
   :open-decision "Whether :implementation should be replaced by target ids from kir/target.cljc, or keep its own names with a declared mapping to them. Until that is decided, an :implementation set is an assertion, not evidence."}
  :conformance-link-debt
  {:as-of "2026-08-12"
   :measured-claims 18
   :entries #{:contextual-document-literal :filter-function :inline-fn-callbacks
              :map-literal :multi-collection-map :persistent-collection-semantics
              :portable-value-model :reduce-function :string-predicate-typing
              :vector-literal}
   :rule "Remove an entry when it gains :conformance or :orphaned-conformance. Do not add one — a new portable claim must name a case."}
